  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Tranquil weather today. A cold front will bring\nthe next opportunity for scattered showers late Sunday into \nMonday. Cooler air again to start the work week. \n\nThe Mid Atlantic region remains in fast zonal flow with a \nstrong upper jet in the area (145 kts). Passing short waves will\ndevelop more of a trough over the region later today, and \ntroughing will encompass most of the eastern CONUS through \nMonday. Dry ridging is expected Tuesday, with a deeper upper low\ndigging towards the Southern Appalachians Wednesday into \nThursday. \n\nBy Sunday night, isolated showers and storms will be moving into\nSE WV/SW VA along a cold front, and these will shift southeast\novernight into Monday. The front looks to arrive a little late \nfor much in the way of thunder, and overnight into Monday \nmorning just expecting light rain, with a few lightning strikes \npossible Monday. No severe weather is expected. QPF looks \npretty pitiful actually, with most spots just hoping to see a \ntenth of an inch out of this...likely due to the energy mostly \nsplitting around the Blue Ridge. \n\nAfter a quite and dry Tuesday, a deeper mid level system and a\nmessy surface low pressure pattern brings scattered convection\nto the region Wednesday through Thursday. With PWATs briefly \njumping close to an inch south of Highway 460, we are hoping \nthis will bring a more widespread beneficial rain.\n\nWhile today will be warmer than yesterday, Sunday looks like the \npick of the week with highs in the mid 70s to low 80s. Another \ncool air mass moves in Monday with overnight lows falling into \nto 30s and low 40s, with frost possible again Tuesday morning \nfor the Greenbrier Valley area. High temperatures will be about \n6-10 degrees below normal through Thursday, with improving \ntemperatures Friday and Saturday.",
